      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND AND PURPOSE",
          "content": "The purpose of this study was to examine whether there is evidence to support 2 elements of the passive-range-of-motion (PROM) portion of Cyriax's selective tissue tension scheme for patients with knee dysfunction: a capsular pattern of motion restriction and the pain-resistance sequence."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SUBJECTS",
          "content": "One hundred fifty-two subjects with unilateral knee dysfunction participated. The subjects had a mean age of 40.0 years (SD=15.9, range=13-82)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Passive range of motion of the knee and the relationship between the onset of pain and resistance to PROM (pain-resistance sequence) were measured, and 4 tests for inflammation were used. Interrater reliability was assessed on 35 subjects."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Kappa values for the individual inflammatory tests ranged from .21 to .66 for categorization of the joint as inflamed, based on at least 2 positive inflammatory tests (kappa=.76). Reliability of PROM measurements was indicated by intraclass correlation coefficients of .72 to .97. Reliability of measurements of the pain-resistance sequence was indicated by a weighted kappa of .28. A capsular pattern, defined as a ratio of loss of extension to loss of flexion during PROM of between 0.03 and 0.50, was more likely than a noncapsular pattern in patients with an inflamed knee or osteoarthrosis (likelihood ratio=3.2). An association was found between a capsular pattern and arthrosis or arthritis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION AND DISCUSSION",
          "content": "These findings provide evidence to support the concept of a capsular pattern of motion restriction in persons with inflamed knees or evidence of osteoarthrosis."
        }
      ]},
